Reject unsorted keys when building a table, in release builds too.

TableBuilder::Add only checks that keys arrive in order under an assert, so a
release build (NDEBUG) writes an unsorted table silently. Nothing downstream
tolerates that: the merging iterator and the DB iterator both skip anything
sorting below what they already emitted, so whole runs of live keys become
invisible to reads while the data sits intact on disk, and every compaction
that reads such a table passes the disorder into its output. See
Nexus-Mods/Vortex#23981, where a full scan returned 167k of 309k live keys.

Failing the build leaves the corrupt table unwritten and surfaces the error on
the flush or compaction that produced it.

RocksDB, leveldb's fork, reached the same design: check_flush_compaction_key_order
shipped in 6.14 defaulting to on, and was removed outright in 9.0.0 with the
check made unconditional, because "over time this validation has shown to be
cheap and correct, so the option to disable it can now be removed". The option
added here follows their first step: on by default, present only so an affected
user can get a writable store back without downgrading, and expected to go once
we have the same confidence.

Note what this means for a store that is ALREADY damaged: the first compaction
that reads an unsorted table fails, DBImpl::RecordBackgroundError latches
bg_error_ for the lifetime of the open DB, and MakeRoomForWrite then yields that
error for every write. Recovery flushes the write-ahead log through the same
path, so this can also fail DB::Open. The store becomes readable but not
writable until it is rebuilt. That is deliberate - a store that silently drops
keys from reads must not keep accepting writes - but it means detection and
repair have to reach such a user, or the option above is their only way out.

The message names both offending keys. RocksDB's equivalent does not, and every
downstream report of it turns into a hunt for which file and which key.

--- a/include/leveldb/options.h
+++ b/include/leveldb/options.h
@@ -57,6 +57,18 @@ struct LEVELDB_EXPORT Options {
   // corruption of one DB entry may cause a large number of entries to
   // become unreadable or for the entire DB to become unopenable.
   bool paranoid_checks = false;

+  // If true, building a table rejects a key that does not sort after the
+  // previous one, failing the flush or compaction that produced it.
+  //
+  // An unsorted table is only partly readable and leveldb reports no error
+  // for it: iterators skip anything sorting below what they already emitted,
+  // so reads silently return less than is stored, and compactions spread the
+  // disorder. Refusing to write one keeps the damage to the operation that
+  // caused it.
+  //
+  // Default: true
+  bool check_key_order = true;
+
   // Use the specified object to interact with the environment,
   // e.g. to read/write files, schedule background work, etc.
   // Default: Env::Default()
--- a/table/table_builder.cc
+++ b/table/table_builder.cc
@@ -15,6 +15,7 @@
 #include "table/format.h"
 #include "util/coding.h"
 #include "util/crc32c.h"
+#include "util/logging.h"

 namespace leveldb {

@@ -96,6 +97,16 @@ void TableBuilder::Add(const Slice& key, const Slice& value) {
   assert(!r->closed);
   if (!ok()) return;
   if (r->num_entries > 0) {
+    // Checked in every build, not just under an assert: an unsorted table is
+    // silently unreadable in part, so refusing to write one is always better
+    // than emitting it.
+    if (r->options.check_key_order &&
+        r->options.comparator->Compare(key, Slice(r->last_key)) <= 0) {
+      r->status = Status::Corruption(
+          "keys added to table out of order",
+          EscapeString(r->last_key) + " then " + EscapeString(key));
+      return;
+    }
     assert(r->options.comparator->Compare(key, Slice(r->last_key)) > 0);
   }

